WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.918
The final lesson of M5 is the richest because it resolves a subtle tension that often frustrates students.

2
00:00:06.918 --> 00:00:13.836
They have learned that heating adds energy, so they expect temperature always to rise strongly when energy enters a sample.

3
00:00:13.836 --> 00:00:20.754
But during a state change, much of the added energy can go into loosening particle links and changing the arrangement rather than sharply raising the average motion.

4
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:27.672
That means the internal energy still rises even if the temperature response is small.

5
00:00:27.672 --> 00:00:34.590
Once students can tell that story clearly, state-change questions stop feeling contradictory and start feeling beautifully consistent.

6
00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:41.508
Keep this takeaway in view: The key gain of the lesson is learning to ask where the new energy went before judging the temperature response.

7
00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:48.426
Heating rule means energy still enters.

8
00:00:48.426 --> 00:00:55.344
Added energy is real even when the temperature change is not dramatic.

9
00:00:55.344 --> 00:01:02.262
State-change rule means links can loosen.

10
00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:09.180
Energy can feed the arrangement or potential-energy part strongly.

11
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:17.849
This diagram matters because it prevents a false all-or-nothing picture.

12
00:01:17.849 --> 00:01:26.517
Added energy is entering the sample, but it does not have to express itself only as faster particle motion.

13
00:01:26.517 --> 00:01:35.186
Near a state change, much of that energy can go into separating particles more, weakening the old arrangement, and increasing the potential-energy part of the internal-energy store.

14
00:01:35.186 --> 00:01:43.854
That is why the temperature may change only a little while the internal-energy rise is still real and substantial.

15
00:01:43.854 --> 00:01:52.523
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong state-change visual lets students see that one incoming energy stream can be split across different internal-energy effects.

16
00:01:52.523 --> 00:02:01.191
Energy input: Heating still transfers energy into the sample during the state change.

17
00:02:01.191 --> 00:02:09.860
Motion response: The average-motion or temperature rise may be limited compared with the energy supplied.

18
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:15.387
A helpful analogy is a budget that can be spent in two ways at once.

19
00:02:15.387 --> 00:02:20.915
One part can go into making a crowd shake more energetically.

20
00:02:20.915 --> 00:02:26.442
Another part can go into unlocking gates so the crowd can rearrange itself more freely.

21
00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:31.969
During a state change, a larger share of the incoming budget may be spent on unlocking rather than on shaking.

22
00:02:31.969 --> 00:02:37.496
That means the whole budget still increases the system's store, but the shake meter alone does not tell the whole story.

23
00:02:37.496 --> 00:02:43.024
This is why temperature and internal energy must be separated carefully in the final lesson.

24
00:02:43.024 --> 00:02:48.551
Keep this takeaway in view: The budget analogy gives students a practical way to picture energy routing during a state change.

25
00:02:48.551 --> 00:02:54.078
Budget route one means more shaking.

26
00:02:54.078 --> 00:02:59.605
Some of the input can raise the average motion and temperature.

27
00:02:59.605 --> 00:03:05.133
Budget route two means more unlocking.

28
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:10.660
Some of the input can loosen links and change the arrangement.

29
00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:16.424
The first statement is that an increase in internal energy can come from energy transferred by heating.

30
00:03:16.424 --> 00:03:22.188
The second is that during a state change, the added energy can raise potential energy more than temperature.

31
00:03:22.188 --> 00:03:27.952
Together, those two rules explain the whole lesson.

32
00:03:27.952 --> 00:03:33.715
Heating does not switch off during a state change.

33
00:03:33.715 --> 00:03:39.479
Internal energy still rises.

34
00:03:39.479 --> 00:03:45.243
But the energy route changes.

35
00:03:45.243 --> 00:03:51.007
Much of the input can go into arrangement and link loosening rather than sharply boosting average kinetic energy.

36
00:03:51.007 --> 00:03:56.771
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula section is strongest when students hear it as an energy-routing explanation rather than a memorized exception.

37
00:03:56.771 --> 00:04:02.535
If energy enters: Internal energy can still increase even when the pulse meter changes only a little.

38
00:04:02.535 --> 00:04:08.298
If links loosen: The potential-energy part of the store is changing significantly.

39
00:04:08.298 --> 00:04:14.062
increase in internal energy = energy transferred by heating.

40
00:04:14.062 --> 00:04:19.826
during a state change, added energy can raise potential energy more than temperature.

41
00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:25.590
The best explanations name where the added energy is going instead of assuming it must mostly raise temperature.

42
00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:31.501
Use a three-step routine.

43
00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:37.412
First, confirm that energy is being transferred into the sample by heating.

44
00:04:37.412 --> 00:04:43.323
Second, ask whether the sample is near or in a state change so that arrangement and link loosening may be important.

45
00:04:43.323 --> 00:04:49.234
Third, decide how much of the energy is likely appearing as higher average motion and how much is likely appearing as changed arrangement.

46
00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:55.145
This route-first routine makes the final lesson feel logical instead of surprising.

47
00:04:55.145 --> 00:05:01.056
Keep this takeaway in view: The route-first routine gives state-change questions a calm structure that protects them from oversimplified answers.

48
00:05:01.056 --> 00:05:06.967
Step 1 means energy enters.

49
00:05:06.967 --> 00:05:12.878
Do not lose the heating input just because the temperature response is modest.

50
00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:18.789
Step 2 means check state boundary.

51
00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:24.700
A state change is the clue that arrangement energy may be taking a large share.

52
00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:28.920
Try four checks.

53
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.140
If energy is supplied during a state change, can internal energy still increase?

54
00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.360
Yes.

55
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.580
If the temperature rises only a little, does that prove almost no energy entered?

56
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.800
No.

57
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.020
Can a large share of the energy go into changing the arrangement or potential-energy part?

58
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:54.240
Yes.

59
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.460
And is it safer to ask where the energy went before judging the size of the temperature response?

60
00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:02.680
Absolutely.

61
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.900
Those are the core habits the final worked example needs.

62
00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.120
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain state-change heating without expecting temperature alone to tell the whole story.

63
00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.340
Energy input means still real.

64
00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.560
Heating continues to transfer energy into the sample.

65
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.780
Temperature response means may be small.

66
00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.000
A modest temperature rise does not mean the process is energetically weak.

67
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:35.527
You are ready for the final worked example now.

68
00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:43.055
Start by stating that heating transfers energy into the sample and increases internal energy.

69
00:06:43.055 --> 00:06:50.582
Then ask whether the energy is mainly raising average motion, mainly loosening links, or doing both.

70
00:06:50.582 --> 00:06:58.109
If the question sits near a state change, be ready to explain why a small temperature response can still accompany a significant rise in internal energy.

71
00:06:58.109 --> 00:07:05.636
The strongest answers in this lesson do not just say what changed; they explain where the added energy went and why that matters.

72
00:07:05.636 --> 00:07:13.164
Keep this takeaway in view: The final worked example is ready: the learner can now track heating through a state change without collapsing internal energy into temperature alone.

73
00:07:13.164 --> 00:07:20.691
Input move: Never let a small temperature change erase the fact that energy was supplied.

74
00:07:20.691 --> 00:07:28.218
State move: Use the state-change context to justify why arrangement energy may dominate.

75
00:07:28.218 --> 00:07:35.745
state that heating transfers energy into the sample.

76
00:07:35.745 --> 00:07:43.273
check whether the process is near or in a state change.

77
00:07:43.273 --> 00:07:50.800
A complete final answer names the energy route before using the temperature clue.
